#!/usr/bin/bash
# =============================================================================
# /usr/libexec/ogc/os-update — "no, Steam, you do not update this computer"
# =============================================================================
# WHAT THIS IS, AND WHY IT IS THREE LINES LONG
#
# In Game Mode, Steam believes it is running on SteamOS, and SteamOS lets Steam
# update the operating system itself — there is an "Check for updates" entry in
# Steam's own settings that expects to be able to do it.
#
# On AquariusOS that must not happen, ever. This operating system is updated by
# downloading a whole new image, all at once, so that it can be rolled back in
# one command if anything is wrong — `aq update`, or the Aquarius logo menu.
# Half an update applied by Steam in the middle of a game is precisely the kind
# of thing that model exists to make impossible.
#
# ⚠️ SO WE DO NOT HIDE THE BUTTON; WE ANSWER IT HONESTLY. The Game Mode package
# ships a script called `steamos-update` which Steam calls, and that script
# hands the real work to this file. Steam's contract with it is exact and is
# written down in the script itself:
#
#     exit 7  =  "there is no update available"
#     exit 0  =  "there is one, here it comes"
#
# So this exits 7, always. Steam shows "your system is up to date", asks nothing
# further, and nothing on the disk is touched. The person updates AquariusOS the
# normal way, from the desktop.
#
# The one thing this must never do is exit 0, which would make Steam start an
# update that cannot happen and sit at a progress bar for ever.
#
# Plain-language guide: docs/restart/game-mode.md
# =============================================================================
echo "AquariusOS updates itself in one piece, from the desktop — not from Steam."
echo "Use 'aq update', or the Aquarius logo menu > Check for Update."
exit 7
